It's quite unusual for linguists to ask "What meanings does a language
need to be able to express?". Normally they ask "What meanings does
(expression Y in) language X express?" or "How do languages in 
general handle the semantic field of such and such?".

So Jboske is doing something unusual but not necessarily avantgarde.
However, a CLL-calibre exposition of Jboske would make a splended
semantics textbook: many many people are interested in the question
"what meanings does a lg need to be able to express/encode?", and
jboske & indeed Loglan in general has a genuine contribution to
make to answering it.

Some debate, such as which meaning belongs to which cmavo, is
irrelevant to addressing that question, but on the other hand
the fact that the insights into meaning get embodied in a
concrete language is probably useful as a way of representing
a map of meanings.

